"A feature store flow diagram distinguishes feature definition, historical retrieval, online materialization, and prediction-time lookup.\n\nUse when: Review consistency between training and serving features. Explain point-in-time historical retrieval and online serving paths.\n\nAvoid when: The model has no shared feature retrieval or materialization boundary.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish feature definitions, offline data, online stores, and consumers. Arrows label historical retrieval separately from online lookup and materialization. Entity-key and timestamp annotations describe how feature rows are matched.\n\nBuild: Choose an entity and a small set of feature definitions. Draw historical training retrieval with event-time alignment. Add materialization into online storage and prediction lookup. Separate offline and online paths into padded lanes with clear cross-lane arrows.\n\nCheck: Training retrieval avoids future information leakage. Online freshness and missing-value behavior are explicit. Entity identifiers agree across ingestion and lookup. Feature labels, timestamps, and lane borders remain unobstructed.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming feature reuse guarantees equal training and serving values. Drawing an online store as the complete historical source.\n\nExample: Customer purchase features are retrieved historically for training and materialized for checkout-time scoring.\n\nAlternatives: Machine-learning pipeline diagram, Data lineage diagram."
